 "grid_note": "HOW THE CELLS WERE LOCATED, because a mark one column off is a person of the wrong age and the method has to be inspectable. The 39 vertical rules were fitted as a single pitch off the PRINTED HEADING band of the same image — where the boxed column labels rule strongly enough to be found — by least squares over every rule the band gives up, and the fit was checked against the heading itself: column 1 must read `Under 5` and column 27 `Under 10` under FREE COLORED MALES before a single cell is read. The sheet is scanned with a LEAN, and the lean is measured rather than assumed: the same 39-rule model is re-fitted every 250 px down the page and the drift read off, which on this deposit runs between -30 px and +18 px from heading to footing — up to half a column, and it has the opposite SIGN on different images of the same book. A grid carried over from a neighbouring page is therefore wrong by half a column at the foot, which is exactly how a mark lands in the next age band; T-0534's first reading of printed 231 was made on such a carried-over grid and its bottom third had to be read again. The 30 or 31 ruled lines were fitted the same way and checked against the committed names, block by block, with the name column rendered beside the cells. Every cell was then read as an image at the fitted grid, with the column number drawn on the crop. No cell value here is an OCR output.",
 "cells_state": "partial",
 "cells_note": "A COLUMN IS COMMITTED ONLY WHERE IT BALANCES: the lines this pass read must SUM to the figure the enumerator wrote at the foot of his own sheet, and a column that does not is left unreconciled with its residual stated rather than forced, because a half-checked column is worse than an unread one. `cells` carries only the balanced columns. `cells_first_pass` carries the whole reading, balanced or not, so the next reader starts from a reading rather than from the sheet — it is NOT reconciled data and nothing downstream may treat it as such. A blank cell on the schedule IS a zero and is recorded by absence; no line was skipped. 37 of 38 columns balance the enumerator's own footings exactly, on all 31 lines. The one that does not is column 15 (free white females 5 under 10), whose footing is a heavy ink blot: the most likely reading of it is 9, which is exactly what the 31 lines sum to, but a figure that cannot be read to better than `low` cannot certify a column, so it is left uncommitted with the coincidence recorded rather than spent.",
